  I expected his hasEventName key to over-report: it is built from the censused stream event
  names, and one of them is `text`, an ordinary English word. Measured over the segment, at
  HIS spelling (case-sensitive) and at the case-insensitive variant my own first transcription
  of it used:
    /\btext\b/  (his spelling)      over 191 bullets: 0 hit(s)
    /\btext\b/i (my transcription)  over 191 bullets: 1 hit(s)
    /\bcompaction\b/i                over 191 bullets: 7 hit(s)
      0.132.0 | * **api:** mark the Text Completions API as deprecated ([2b75b49](https://github.com/anthropics/anth
  [PRED] LANDED   his `text` leg over-reports AT HIS OWN SPELLING (my hypothesis): expected 0, measured 0
  [GATE] OK   CONTROL the /\btext\b/ key can fire at all (driven on a known positive, his spelling)
  -> REFUTED. His figure is not inflated by that leg, and the reason is one character:
     hasEventName is the ONE key in his instrument built without an `i` flag
     (`new RegExp(`\\b${e}\\b`)`), while every sibling key carries one.

  But the near-miss is worth the sentence, because his own exclusion could not have caught it.
  The single case-insensitive hit is an `**api:**` bullet about the Text Completions API — a
  surface his FOREIGN-SURFACE bucket keys `\bcompletions\b` specifically to exclude. That
  bucket sits at precedence 9; STREAM-EVENT-NAME sits at 7. So with an `i` flag the row would
  be claimed as Klatch-reachable BEFORE the exclusion that exists for it is ever consulted:
